{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // Empty rather than absent: nothing under this package reaches a Node
    // global, and the default is to pull in every `@types` package the
    // workspace install happens to have hoisted, which would let a global
    // resolve here that no dependency of this package provides.
    "types": [],
    // `@/*` -> `src/*`, matching the alias `astro.config.ts` registers for
    // Vite and the alias `components.json` declares for shadcn's tooling. Only
    // for `tsc`'s own resolution; the build's is the Vite alias above.
    // `@playdeck/react` is stated rather than left to node resolution, for
    // `include`'s one out-of-package file below: a module specifier resolves
    // from the importing file's own directory, and `examples/` sits outside
    // this app, so the walk up from `examples/react-menus.tsx` never reaches
    // `apps/site/node_modules`, where the workspace link lives.
    // `apps/storybook/tsconfig.json` carries the same block for the same
    // reason. The target is the built declarations, which is what node
    // resolution already reaches through the package's `exports`, and the
    // reference below is what makes `tsc -b` build them first.
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@playdeck/react": ["../../packages/react/dist/index.d.ts"]
    },
    // Outside the package, unlike `apps/storybook`, which writes its own to
    // `dist/`. Here `dist/` is Astro's *output* directory: `astro build` clears
    // it, so a buildinfo kept there is destroyed by every build, and one written
    // after a build is copied into the published artifact by
    // `scripts/assemble-deploy.mjs`. `scripts/tsconfig.json` already keeps its
    // buildinfo here for the analogous reason.
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "../../node_modules/.cache/tsbuildinfo/site.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  // `tsc` cannot parse `.astro` files, so this covers the TypeScript the site
  // actually has and never `**/*.astro`: naming them here would fail
  // `pnpm typecheck` on syntax it has no parser for. `src/**/*.ts` is here so a
  // module the site grows is checked without anyone remembering to come back
  // (#519).
  //
  // What that leaves is a real gap and not a handoff: nothing in this
  // repository type-checks the `.astro` templates or the inline scripts inside
  // them. There is no `astro check` here — no dependency, no script, no CI step
  // — so the frontmatter and the `<script>` blocks are compiled by the bundler
  // and never checked. `astro build` renders every page, which catches a
  // template that names something undefined at module scope, and it is the only
  // gate over those files; a `ReferenceError` inside an event handler in
  // `index.astro` reached a commit on this branch precisely because rendering is
  // not checking.
  //
  // `.astro/` — the directory `astro sync` writes its generated ambient types
  // into — is left out for the same reason it is gitignored: it is build output,
  // it does not exist on a clean checkout, and a project reference that only
  // typechecks after a build has run is a broken reference.
  //
  // `.tsx` is here for the site's one React island. It is real TypeScript that
  // `tsc` can parse, so it belongs in this pass rather than being left to the
  // Astro tooling that has to handle the templates.
  //
  // `examples/react-menus.tsx` is named one by one rather than swept in with a
  // glob, the way `apps/storybook/tsconfig.json` names the two archetype
  // examples: `ControlBar` in `BenchIsland.tsx` mounts its `RateMenu`, and this
  // project is `composite`, so a file it compiles but was not told about is a
  // hard error ("File is not listed within the file list of project"). It
  // cannot be a project *reference* either, since `examples/` is `noEmit`
  // (TS6310, the same error `tsconfig.test.json`'s own comment names).
  "include": [
    "astro.config.ts",
    "src/**/*.ts",
    "src/**/*.tsx",
    "../../examples/react-menus.tsx"
  ],
  // The island imports `@playdeck/react`, which resolves to declarations that
  // package emits rather than to its source, so this project cannot be checked
  // until that one has been built. Declaring the reference is what makes
  // `tsc -b` build it first instead of failing on a missing file.
  // `bench-composition.ts` adds the same need for `@playdeck/core`.
  "references": [
    { "path": "../../packages/react" },
    { "path": "../../packages/core" }
  ],
  // The one file that cannot be covered by the line above, and it is the
  // exclusion above that puts it here rather than anything about the file. It
  // imports `astro:content`, a virtual module whose declarations exist only in
  // the generated `.astro/` directory this project deliberately does not read,
  // so `tsc` resolves the import to nothing and fails on a file that is
  // perfectly well-formed. Nothing else checks it either — see the gap named
  // above — so what stands behind this file is `astro build` running its
  // loaders, and the reference pages being empty if they are wrong.
  "exclude": ["src/content.config.ts"]
}
